WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.376
Symmetric encryption uses the same secret key for protecting data and later recovering it.

2
00:00:05.376 --> 00:00:13.824
It is common for stored records, local files, service messages, and other cases where both sides are allowed to share a secret.

3
00:00:13.824 --> 00:00:16.128
Confidentiality alone is not always enough.

4
00:00:16.128 --> 00:00:22.656
If encrypted data can be modified without detection, the application may process data it should not trust.

5
00:00:22.656 --> 00:00:29.568
Modern designs usually prefer authenticated encryption so confidentiality and integrity are handled together by a library-supported mode.

6
00:00:29.568 --> 00:00:32.256
Authenticated encryption can also use associated data.

7
00:00:32.256 --> 00:00:37.248
Associated data is not encrypted, but it is bound to the authentication result.

8
00:00:37.248 --> 00:00:45.696
This can be useful for context such as version, tenant, purpose, record identifier, or protocol metadata that must match the protected content.

9
00:00:45.696 --> 00:00:48.768
The application should reject unauthenticated data before use.

10
00:00:48.768 --> 00:00:54.912
Do not parse, deserialize, route, or act on protected content until the library reports successful authentication.

11
00:00:54.912 --> 00:01:02.208
Failure should be treated as a normal security-relevant outcome, not as an exceptional path that is rarely tested.

12
00:01:02.208 --> 00:01:06.432
Unique nonces or IVs may be required by the chosen mode.

13
00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:12.576
The application should follow the library documentation exactly and make nonce policy visible in code review.

14
00:01:12.576 --> 00:01:17.568
Avoid assembling encryption and authentication manually unless an approved design explicitly requires it.

15
00:01:17.568 --> 00:01:22.944
Combining primitives safely is subtle, and mature libraries already provide modes that reduce misuse.

16
00:01:22.944 --> 00:01:31.008
A reviewable symmetric-encryption design identifies the key source, nonce strategy, associated data, failure behavior, and the exact point where data

17
00:01:31.008 --> 00:01:33.312
becomes trusted enough for application use.

18
00:01:33.312 --> 00:01:36.768
It should also identify what is intentionally not encrypted.

19
00:01:36.768 --> 00:01:43.680
Associated data, headers, and routing fields may remain visible, so the design should confirm that visibility is acceptable.

20
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:51.360
Operationally, encryption code should be tested with valid data, modified data, wrong context, missing keys, and version transitions so failure

21
00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.280
handling is not improvised later.
